According to different impedance spectra indicated in Fig.  6 , a one-time constant can be considered for the annealed and nano-grained AA2024 EIS spectra. Hence, the electrical equivalent circuit (EEC) represented in Fig.  6  can be considered for simulating and modeling the experimental impedance spectra. This EEC was previously used by Fatah-alhosseini et al. 48  to describe the passive behavior of AA2024 in borate buffer solution. It should be noted that the single time-constant model is the ideal case against which real systems are compared. It assumes (i) a perfectly smooth, homogeneous electrode surface, (ii) a single, uncomplicated charge-transfer reaction with no adsorbed intermediates, (iii) a double layer that behaves as a perfect, frequency-independent capacitor, and (iv) semi-infinite linear diffusion (which, importantly, is not in the Randles circuit itself; the classic Randles circuit uses a Warburg element in series with Rct to model diffusion, creating a two-time-constant circuit). Therefore, its use is justified when the system under investigation approximates these ideal conditions or as a first-order approximation to understand the primary bottleneck 91 , 92 . The elements of the EEC are introduced as follows: R s  marks solution resistance, R p  accounts for the passive film resistance, and Qpf is the constant phase element (CPE) corresponding to the oxide passive film capacitance. For CPE, the impedance and the capacitance can be calculated by Eqs. ( 1 ) and ( 2 ), respectively 40 , 41 . 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
